% 36e-retargeting.weft — The Porting Playbook: adding an architecture
%
% Pure prose. This chapter is the design rationale AND the step-by-step
% recipe for retargeting kcc to a new architecture. No code is emitted
% here; the concrete target modules are written when a target is added.

\chapter{Retargeting \kcc{}: The Porting Playbook}
\label{ch:retargeting}

This chapter is a promise to a future contributor.  When a new
architecture is to be supported---x86-64, RISC-V, a second ARM ABI, a
future ISA that does not yet exist---this is the document to read first.
It explains \emph{why} \kcc{} is organised so that a new back end is cheap,
\emph{what} exactly must be supplied, and \emph{in what order} to build it
so that every step is small and testable.  The intent is blunt: after
reading this chapter, a competent implementer should be able to open a
processor manual and an ABI document and start writing the new target the
same afternoon.  Breadth---supporting as many architectures as
possible---is a first-class goal, and the only way to reach it is to make
each additional architecture a matter of \emph{filling in a form}, not
inventing a compiler.

\section{The Principle: One Front End, Many Back Ends}
\label{sec:one-front-many-back}

Everything in \kcc{} is arranged around a single dividing line: the
intermediate representation (Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-design}).  Above that line
the compiler is \emph{target-neutral}; below it, target-specific.  This is
not an accident of style---it is the entire strategy for portability.

Above the IR sits the whole language: the lexer, the preprocessor, the
parser, semantic analysis, and IR construction.  None of it knows or cares
what machine will eventually run the program.  It is written once and never
touched again when a target is added.  So is the optimizer
(Chapters~\ref{ch:ir-passes}--\ref{sec:dce}): constant folding, dead-code
elimination, common-subexpression elimination, and scalar-local promotion
all operate on the IR, and are therefore shared by every target for free.
So, crucially, is the \emph{logic} of register allocation
(\S\ref{sec:regalloc-portable} below): linear scan, liveness, the loop and
call analyses, the mem2reg reinterpretation---all of it is neutral, and
only the \emph{register banks} it draws from are target data.

Below the IR sits exactly what differs between machines: how an IR
operation becomes instructions, which registers exist and how they are
partitioned, how arguments are passed, how a stack frame is laid out, and
how the result is turned into an object file the platform's linker
accepts.  A new target supplies these---and nothing else.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Written once (target-neutral) & Written per target \\
\hline
lexer, preprocessor, parser & instruction selection (IR op $\to$ asm) \\
semantic analysis, type system & register banks (names, counts, roles) \\
IR construction and validation & ABI (argument/return, alignment) \\
optimizer (fold, DCE, CSE, mem2reg) & prologue/epilogue and frame layout \\
register-allocation \emph{logic} & assembler and linker invocation \\
diagnostics, driver skeleton & runtime (syscalls, \texttt{\_start}) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The left column is roughly seventy percent of the compiler and never grows
when architectures are added.  The right column is what this chapter is
about.

\section{Two Ways to Reach Metal}
\label{sec:two-ways}

There are two honest ways to turn a stream of instructions into an object
file, and \kcc{} deliberately supports both.

The first is \emph{direct emission}: the back end computes the exact bytes
of each instruction and writes the object file itself.  This is what the
ARM64 back end does---Chapter~\ref{ch:emit-arm64} encodes every instruction
class into its 32-bit word, and Chapter~\ref{ch:emit-macho} lays out the
Mach-O container by hand.  It owns everything down to the last bit, depends
on nothing but the system linker, and is the truest expression of \kcc{}'s
``build your own floor'' character.  Its price is that each instruction-set
needs a full encoder---an intricate, error-prone module---and each object
format needs its own writer.

The second is \emph{assembly emission}: the back end prints textual
assembly (\texttt{.s}) and hands it to the platform's assembler, which
produces the object file.  The assembler does the encoding \emph{and} emits
the native object format---ELF on Linux, Mach-O on macOS, COFF on
Windows---so a target that emits assembly needs \emph{neither} an
instruction encoder \emph{nor} an object writer.  What remains is only the
interesting, machine-defining work: choosing which instructions to emit,
allocating registers, and obeying the ABI.

For \emph{growing to many architectures}, the second way is decisively more
economical, and the reason is structural, not merely a matter of typing
less.  An instruction encoder is the part of a back end most likely to
harbour subtle, hard-to-find bugs (a wrong bit in a rarely-used addressing
mode), and it must be written and hardened \emph{per instruction set}.  An
object writer must be written \emph{per object format}.  Delegating both to
the system assembler means inheriting decades of hardened correctness
instead of maintaining a new bug surface for every ISA and every format.
That is not a compromise for a production tool; it is the sensible choice.
Many production compilers emit assembly for exactly this reason.

It is worth being precise about what is delegated and what is kept, because
the distinction is the whole argument.  \kcc{} keeps everything that makes
it a compiler: the front end, the IR, the optimizer, register allocation,
instruction \emph{selection}, and the ABI.  It delegates only the two most
mechanical, most target-multiplied, least conceptually interesting steps:
turning chosen instructions into bytes, and packing bytes into an object
file.  A target built this way is not ``a front end to the assembler''---it
is a complete compiler that uses the platform assembler as its code-object
back end, which is a respectable and common architecture.

The flagship ARM64 target remains direct: it already exists, it is the
proof that \kcc{} can go all the way to the metal from scratch, and it is
what makes the self-hosting build byte-identical.  We do not rewrite it.
The target interface (\S\ref{sec:target-interface}) is what lets the two
coexist: each target chooses its own emission in its own \emph{emit}
operation.  ARM64 emits bytes and a Mach-O; a new target emits text and
invokes \texttt{as}.  Nothing above the emit operation knows the
difference.  Should a direct encoder for a new ISA ever be wanted (for the
same from-scratch pride, or to drop the assembler dependency), it can be
added later as a different \emph{emit} without disturbing selection, the
allocator, or the ABI---so choosing assembly now forecloses nothing.

\section{The Target Interface}
\label{sec:target-interface}

A target is a \emph{descriptor} plus a set of \emph{operations}.  The
descriptor is data; the operations are the code a new back end supplies.
Adding an architecture means creating one target module that provides all
of them, and registering it so that \texttt{--target} can select it.

\paragraph{Identity and toolchain.}  The descriptor first fixes the basics:
a name (\texttt{arm64}, \texttt{x86\_64}, \ldots), the pointer width and
endianness, the symbol-name convention (the Mach-O world prefixes an
underscore; ELF does not), the assembler command and flags to run on the
emitted \texttt{.s}, the object format it produces, and the linker command
and startup files used to produce an executable.  For a direct target the
assembler command is empty and the object writer is used instead.

\paragraph{Register banks.}  For each class of register---general-purpose
and floating-point---the target declares, as plain data: the register
names (or numbers) available for allocation, how many there are, which are
\emph{caller-saved} (free across a call only if the value is dead there)
versus \emph{callee-saved} (preserved across calls, but must be spilled and
restored by the prologue/epilogue), and which are reserved by the ABI or
used as fixed scratch by the emitter (the stack pointer, the frame pointer,
the argument registers during a call, any hardwired zero register).  These
banks are the \emph{only} machine-specific input the register allocator
needs; see \S\ref{sec:regalloc-portable}.

\paragraph{The calling convention (ABI).}  The target supplies the rules
for passing and returning values: which registers carry the first several
integer and pointer arguments, which carry floating arguments, how an
argument too large for a register or beyond the register budget is passed
on the stack, how an aggregate is classified (the System V AMD64
eightbyte rules; the AArch64 homogeneous-aggregate and by-reference rules),
where the return value lands, the required stack alignment at a call, and
any red zone.  In \kcc{} this is expressed as a small classification
routine plus a table---not scattered through the code---so that a new ABI
is read out of its specification and transcribed in one place.

\paragraph{Instruction selection.}  This is the heart of a new target: for
each IR operation the emitter produces the equivalent instruction or short
sequence for this machine.  A constant becomes a move-immediate; an
\texttt{IR\_ADD} becomes the machine's add; a \texttt{IR\_LOAD} becomes a
load from the addressing mode the target favours; a comparison plus
conditional branch becomes the machine's compare-and-branch idiom; a call
becomes the argument marshaling from the ABI followed by the call
instruction.  For an assembly target each case simply prints the mnemonic
and operands; for a direct target it encodes the bytes.  Selection is where
the bulk of a new target's lines live, but each case is small, local, and
independently testable.

\paragraph{Prologue, epilogue, and frame.}  The target lays out the stack
frame (spill slots for un-allocated virtual registers, \texttt{alloca}
storage, the callee-saved save area, incoming stack arguments) and emits
the entry sequence that establishes it and the exit sequence that tears it
down and returns.  The frame \emph{shape} is target-specific; the
\emph{contents} (how many vreg slots, which callee-saved registers were
claimed) come from the shared register allocator.

\paragraph{Emission.}  Finally the target says how assembly text is spelled
for this machine---the operand syntax, the directives that open the code
and data sections, how a label and a global symbol are declared---and how
to drive the assembler and linker.  A direct target instead encodes bytes
and writes the object; either way, the operation above it is the same
``produce an object file from this function's instructions.''

\section{The Register Allocator Is Already Portable}
\label{sec:regalloc-portable}

The most valuable thing to understand before porting is that register
allocation does \emph{not} need to be rewritten per target.  The allocator
of Chapter~\ref{ch:codegen} (\S\ref{sec:regalloc}) is a linear scan over
virtual-register live ranges; its liveness computation, its back-edge and
call analyses, its loop-carried reservation, and the mem2reg
reinterpretation of scalar-local allocas are all expressed in terms of the
IR and of \emph{abstract} register slots, not of any particular machine's
registers.  What differs between machines is purely the \emph{banks}: how
many caller-saved and callee-saved integer registers exist, the same for
floating-point, and which physical register each abstract slot maps to.

A new target therefore does not touch the allocator.  It supplies its bank
descriptors---``these four registers are caller-saved scratch, these nine
are callee-saved, the floating bank is these''---and the allocator's
existing machinery produces a map from virtual register to physical
register for that bank.  The two banks (integer and floating) are allocated
by the same logic run over different descriptors, exactly as the ARM64
back end already does with its general and \texttt{d}-register banks.  This
is the single largest reason a new architecture is cheap: the hardest part
of a back end to get right is written once and reused.

\section{The Porting Playbook}
\label{sec:porting-playbook}

The recipe below is ordered so that each step ends at a running program
that does slightly more than the last.  Never build the whole target before
testing any of it; build the smallest thing that runs, confirm it against
the reference compiler, and grow.

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Register the target and its \texttt{--target} dispatch.}
Create the target descriptor with its identity and toolchain fields, and
wire \texttt{--target=\emph{name}} in the driver to select it while leaving
ARM64/Mach-O the default and untouched.  \emph{Milestone:}
\texttt{kcc --target=\emph{name}} runs and reaches the (still empty) back
end without disturbing the existing target.

\item \textbf{Declare the register banks.}  Read the ABI's register roles
and transcribe them: the caller-saved and callee-saved general registers,
the floating registers, and the reserved ones (stack pointer, frame
pointer, the argument registers, any zero register).  Nothing runs yet;
this is data the allocator will consume.

\item \textbf{Stand up emission.}  Emit the assembly-file preamble---the
directives for the code and data sections, the symbol syntax, the
label-and-global declarations---and wire the call to the assembler and
linker.  \emph{Milestone:} \kcc{} emits a syntactically valid, empty
\texttt{.s} that the platform assembler accepts and the linker turns into
an (empty) object.

\item \textbf{Select the minimal instruction set.}  Implement just enough
selection to compile \texttt{int main()\{ return N; \}}: materialise an
integer constant, and the function return.  Add the prologue/epilogue in
its simplest form.  \emph{Milestone:} the program builds, runs (under the
chosen execution method---\S\ref{sec:testing-cross}), and exits with
\texttt{N}.  This is the moment the whole framework is proven end to end.

\item \textbf{Grow selection, one IR opcode at a time.}  Add integer
arithmetic and bitwise operations, then loads and stores, then comparisons
and branches, then unconditional jumps.  After each opcode, compile a small
program exercising it and difftest it against the reference compiler.  A
divergence now is localised to the one opcode just added, which is why the
order matters.

\item \textbf{Implement the calling convention.}  Marshal arguments into
their registers and stack slots, and read the return value from its
register, per the ABI declared in step~2.  \emph{Milestone:} multi-argument
calls work, and \texttt{printf} produces correct output---the first
programs that look like real code.

\item \textbf{Complete the frame and the wide cases.}  Handle \texttt{alloca}
storage, spilled virtual registers, more than the register budget of
arguments (stack arguments), and large frames.  \emph{Milestone:} functions
with many locals and deep expressions match the reference compiler.

\item \textbf{Add floating point.}  Declare the floating register bank (the
allocator handles it with the same logic), select the floating arithmetic
and conversion instructions, and implement the floating half of the ABI.
\emph{Milestone:} the floating-point programs in the difference corpus
pass.

\item \textbf{Provide the runtime and finish the toolchain.}  Supply the
target's system-call convention and \texttt{\_start} for freestanding
programs, and the exact linker invocation (startup files, libraries) for
the platform.  \emph{Milestone:} \texttt{kcc file.c -o out} produces a
runnable executable in one step, as on the flagship target.

\end{enumerate}

At the end of this ladder the new target passes the same difference corpus
the flagship does.  Each rung was small, and each was verified before the
next was begun.

\paragraph{As realized for x86-64.}  This ladder is not hypothetical: it is
exactly the path the x86-64 back end of Chapter~\ref{ch:emit-x86} walked---and
walked to the end.  Rung one stood up emission and the minimal instruction set
together (\texttt{const}\,+\,\texttt{ret}, proving the pipeline with
\texttt{int main(void)\{return N;\}}); rung two grew selection across the whole
integer ALU and the memory operations over an all-spill frame; rung three added
comparisons, branches, and the System~V calling convention, including
stack-passed arguments; the later rungs completed the frame, the data section,
floating point, and the runtime.  Each was committed only once its programs ran
exit-identically to \texttt{clang -arch x86\_64} and the ARM64 oracle stayed
byte-identical to the last bit.

\paragraph{A complete second target.}  What began as one rung is now a full
second target.  x86-64 emits AT\&T assembly from the same neutral IR and lets
the system \texttt{as} and \texttt{ld} encode and link it, on both Mach-O
(macOS) and ELF (Linux); it implements the whole System~V AMD64 ABI---integer
and floating arguments, variadic \texttt{va\_list}, stack-passed and by-value
aggregate arguments, computed \texttt{goto}---and passes the difference corpus.
It compiles real, unmodified software: Lua, SQLite, and Linux-kernel
translation units---one of which, a \kcc{}-compiled \texttt{init/main.o}, boots
a tiny-config Linux~6.6 to the point of executing \texttt{init}.  On Linux the
target \emph{self-hosts}: \kcc{} compiled by \kcc{} reproduces every one of its
own modules to a three-way byte-identical fixpoint, and drives \texttt{as} and
\texttt{ld} itself, so that \texttt{kcc file.c -o out} yields an ELF executable
in a single command.  Throughout, the code generation is entirely \kcc{}'s
work; Rosetta on the Mac and a user-mode emulator on Linux only \emph{run} the
output so it can be checked against the oracle (\S\ref{sec:testing-cross}), and
the ARM64 target stayed byte-identical to the bit.  The playbook is therefore
not a hope but a demonstrated path: a back end built to its shape reaches all
the way to real, running software---not a subset.

\section{A Target Skeleton}
\label{sec:target-skeleton}

The interface of \S\ref{sec:target-interface} and the ladder of
\S\ref{sec:porting-playbook} describe the work; this section names it as a
concrete list of artifacts to create, in the shape they take inside \kcc{}.
Treat the x86-64 back end (Chapter~\ref{ch:emit-x86}) as the worked template:
every item below already exists there in full, and the fastest way to write a
new target is to read x86-64's version of each and transcribe it for the new
machine.  Nothing above the emitter is touched.

\paragraph{1. Selection and identity.}  Add the architecture to the
\texttt{\_kcc\_target} selector and route \texttt{--target=\emph{name}} to it in
the driver, leaving ARM64/Mach-O the default.  Fix the symbol convention (an
underscore prefix for the Mach-O world, none for ELF) and, if the target serves
two object formats, an OS selector---\texttt{\_kcc\_x86\_os} is the precedent
for a single emitter that produces both Mach-O and ELF.

\paragraph{2. Register banks (pure data).}  Declare, as arrays, the
allocatable general registers split into caller-saved and callee-saved, the
floating bank likewise, and the reserved registers (stack pointer, frame
pointer, the argument registers, any zero register).  A legal shortcut for
bring-up is to declare \emph{no} allocatable registers and spill every value to
the frame; this is exactly how x86-64 first came up, and it lets selection be
written and tested before the banks are tuned.

\paragraph{3. ABI classifier and argument marshaller.}  Extend the type
classifier (\texttt{\_kcc\_ir\_classify\_type}) with this machine's rules for
where each value is passed and returned, and the marshaller
(\texttt{\_kcc\_cg\_marshal\_args}) that places arguments in their registers and
stack slots and reads the result back.  This is the ABI, transcribed from its
specification in one place rather than scattered through selection.

\paragraph{4. Instruction selection.}  One case per IR opcode, printing the
mnemonic and operands for this machine.  This is the bulk of the new lines but
each case is small and independently testable; x86-64's per-opcode emitter is
the direct model.

\paragraph{5. Prologue, epilogue, and frame.}  Lay out the stack frame---spill
slots, \texttt{alloca} storage, the callee-saved save area, incoming stack
arguments---and emit the entry and exit sequences.  The \emph{shape} is yours;
the \emph{contents} (slot count, which callee-saved registers were claimed)
come from the shared allocator (\S\ref{sec:regalloc-portable}).

\paragraph{6. Emission and toolchain.}  Spell the assembly preamble (section
directives, label and global syntax), then invoke the system \texttt{as} to
encode it and \texttt{ld} to link it, with the platform's startup files.  A
direct target instead encodes bytes and writes the object itself, as ARM64 does
(Chapters~\ref{ch:emit-arm64}--\ref{ch:emit-macho}).

\paragraph{7. Runtime.}  Supply the target's system-call convention and a
\texttt{\_start} for freestanding programs, so the driver can turn a source
file into a runnable executable in one command.

\section{A Porting Checklist}
\label{sec:porting-checklist}

Each rung of the playbook pairs one deliverable with one proof.  Do them in
order, and never begin a rung until the previous one runs exit-identically to
the oracle (\S\ref{sec:testing-cross}); a divergence is then localised to the
single rung just added.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}p{2.1cm}p{5.1cm}p{5.1cm}@@{}}
\hline
Rung & What you write & How you prove it \\
\hline
1. Dispatch & target added to \texttt{\_kcc\_target}; \texttt{--target} wired &
\texttt{kcc --target=\emph{name}} reaches the back end; ARM64 default unchanged \\
2. Banks & register-bank arrays (or none, all-spill) &
compiles; the allocator consumes the banks \\
3. Emission & \texttt{.s} preamble \,+\, \texttt{as}/\texttt{ld} wiring &
an empty \texttt{.s} assembles and links to an object \\
4. Minimal ISA & materialise a constant, the return, the simplest prologue &
\texttt{int main(void)\{return N;\}} exits with \texttt{N} \\
5. Grow ISA & ALU, then loads/stores, then compares/branches, then jumps &
one difftest program per opcode passes \\
6. Calls & argument marshalling and the return value &
multi-argument calls and \texttt{printf} match the oracle \\
7. Frame & \texttt{alloca}, spills, stack arguments, large frames &
many-locals and deep-expression programs match \\
8. Floating & FP bank, FP selection, the FP half of the ABI &
the floating-point difference corpus passes \\
9. Runtime & syscalls, \texttt{\_start}, the exact link line &
\texttt{kcc file.c -o out} runs in one step \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

When the last row is green the new target passes the same difference corpus the
flagship does, and every row before it was verified in isolation.  That is the
whole promise of the seam: retargeting is transcription against a checklist, not
invention.

\section{Testing a Cross Target}
\label{sec:testing-cross}

\kcc{} itself is an ARM64 executable; a new target's output runs somewhere
else, so testing is cross-compilation checked against an oracle.  The
oracle is the reference compiler (\texttt{clang}) asked to cross-compile the
same source for the same target; the difference harness compiles a program
with both \kcc{} and the oracle, runs both outputs, and compares.  Only the
\emph{execution} of the output differs from the native flow: an x86-64
Mach-O output runs directly under the Apple-silicon binary translator; an
x86-64 Linux/ELF output runs under a user-mode emulator or on real
hardware.  Choosing a first target whose output can be executed on the
development machine---so the existing difference harness applies with only
the execution command changed---removes an entire category of friction from
the initial bring-up and is strongly preferred for the first new target.

The milestone ladder doubles as the test plan: return value, then
arithmetic, then \texttt{printf}, then loops, then aggregates, then the full
corpus.  Because the front end, IR, and optimizer are shared and already
verified, a divergence on a new target is almost always in the one back-end
step most recently added---which is exactly why the playbook adds one thing
at a time.

\section{What Stays Owned, What Is Borrowed}
\label{sec:owned-borrowed}

To close, the boundary restated, because it is the point of the whole
design.  \kcc{} owns the compiler: the front end that turns C into an IR,
the optimizer that improves the IR, the allocator that places values in
registers, the selection that chooses instructions, and the ABI that makes
functions interoperate.  For non-flagship targets it borrows two things
from the platform assembler---the encoding of instructions into bytes and
the layout of the object file---because those are the parts that multiply
per architecture and per format and that the system toolchain already does
perfectly.  The flagship target owns even those, and stands as the proof
that \kcc{} can build the whole floor when it chooses to.  This split keeps
the from-scratch claim exactly where it is most meaningful and makes breadth
across architectures a matter of transcription rather than invention---which
is what a compiler meant for the present and the future needs.
